{"details":{"initial_reason":"object adds no factual detail beyond the subject","model_output_excerpt":"{\"claim\":{\"subject\":\"Republic of China (1945–present)\",\"relation\":\"recognized as a republic of China\",\"object\":\"the Republic of China (1945–present)\",\"evidence_text\":\"Republic of China (1945–present)\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"question_relevance\":\"direct\",\"valid_from\":null,\"valid_until\":null}}","question":"What is the recognized as a republic of China?","reason":"object adds no factual detail beyond the subject","selected_passage_excerpt":"Section: Republic of China (1945–present)\n\n### Republic of China (1945–present)\n\nMain articles: Republic of China (1912–1949) and History of Taiwan (1945–present)\nSee also: History of the Republic of China and Chinese Civil War\nGeneral Chen Yi (right) accepting the receipt of General Order No.\n1 from Rikichi Andō (left), the last Japanese governor-general of Taiwan, in Taipei City Hall on October 25, 1945.\n\nWhile Taiwan was under Japanese rule, the Republic of China was founded on mainland China on 1 January 1912 following the Xinhai Revolution of 1911.\nCentral authority waxed and waned in response to warlordism (1915–28), Japanese invasion (1937–45), and the Chinese Civil War (1927–49), with central authority strongest during the Nanjing decade (1927–37), when most of China came under the control of the Kuomintang (KMT).\nThe Communists founded the People's Republic of China on 1 October.\nThese people and their descendants became known in Taiwan as waishengren (外省人).\nThe ROC government took to Taipei many national treasures and much of China's gold and foreign currency reserves.\nAlong with Hong Kong, Singapore, and South Korea, Taiwan became known as one of the Four Asian Tigers.\n\nBecause of the Cold War, most Western nations and the United Nations regarded the ROC as the sole legitimate government of China until the 1970s.\nOn 15 July 1987, Chiang lifted martial law on the main island of Tai