{"details":{"initial_reason":"object adds no factual detail beyond the subject","model_output_excerpt":"{\"claim\":{\"subject\":\"China\",\"relation\":\"country\",\"object\":\"China\",\"evidence_text\":\"China\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"question_relevance\":\"direct\",\"valid_from\":null,\"valid_until\":null}}","question":"What is the country of China?","reason":"object adds no factual detail beyond the subject","selected_passage_excerpt":"Section: Ancient China\n\n### Ancient China\n\nA set of bronze ritual vessels arranged in a museum displayA set of Western Zhou-era ritual bronzes, c.\nThese are the earliest known form of writing in China and the ancestor of modern Chinese characters.\n\nThe Shang were overthrown by the Zhou c.\nThe Zhou ruled over a vast and loose confederation of vassal states across central China, which was gradually weakened by regional lords.\n\nSection: Imperial China\n\n### Imperial China\n\nFurther information: Chinese Empire\n\n#### Classical period\n\nA group of the Terracotta Warriors standing in an archaeological dig siteSoldier statues of the Terracotta Army, buried alongside Qin Shi Huang c.\nThe dynasty lasted only fifteen years, falling soon after his death from revolts and civil war.\n\nAfter the collapse, the Han dynasty emerged to rule China between 206 BCE and 220 CE.\nMilitary expeditions against the Xiongnu, a confederation of nomadic steppe tribes, expanded Han influence into parts of Central Asia and helped to establish the Silk Road, allowing for trade connections between China and western Eurasia.\nContemporaneously, merchants established maritime trade routes linking China, Southeast Asia, and India.\nThe dynasty is the namesake of the Han Chinese, China's main ethnic group.\n\nThe Han faced widespread uprisings and the emergence of local warlords in the 100s and 200s CE.\nSinicized formerly-nomadic peoples who had settled in Northern China, such as the Xiongnu, rebelled and founded new dynasties.\nVarious Han Chinese dynasties in Southern China emerged in the 300s.\nDuring these conflicts, Buddhism was introduced to China via the Silk Road.\n\n#### Mid-imperial period\n\nChina was reunited under the Sui dynasty in 589, which constructed the Grand Canal to link Northern and Southern China.","source_title":"China","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_repair","ts_unix_ms":1791215836874}
{"details":{"initial_reason":"object adds no factual detail beyond the subject","initial_status":"ungrounded","model_output_excerpt":"{\"claim\":{\"subject\":\"China\",\"relation\":\"country\",\"object\":\"China\",\"evidence_text\":\"China\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"question_relevance\":\"direct\",\"valid_from\":null,\"valid_until\":null}}","question":"What is the country of China?","reason":"object adds no factual detail beyond the subject","relevance":"direct","selected_passage_excerpt":"Section: Ancient China\n\n### Ancient China\n\nA set of bronze ritual vessels arranged in a museum displayA set of Western Zhou-era ritual bronzes, c.\nThese are the earliest known form of writing in China and the ancestor of modern Chinese characters.\n\nThe Shang were overthrown by the Zhou c.\nThe Zhou ruled over a vast and loose confederation of vassal states across central China, which was gradually weakened by regional lords.\n\nSection: Imperial China\n\n### Imperial China\n\nFurther information: Chinese Empire\n\n#### Classical period\n\nA group of the Terracotta Warriors standing in an archaeological dig siteSoldier statues of the Terracotta Army, buried alongside Qin Shi Huang c.\nThe dynasty lasted only fifteen years, falling soon after his death from revolts and civil war.\n\nAfter the collapse, the Han dynasty emerged to rule China between 206 BCE and 220 CE.\nMilitary expeditions against the Xiongnu, a confederation of nomadic steppe tribes, expanded Han influence into parts of Central Asia and helped to establish the Silk Road, allowing for trade connections between China and western Eurasia.\nContemporaneously, merchants established maritime trade routes linking China, Southeast Asia, and India.\nThe dynasty is the namesake of the Han Chinese, China's main ethnic group.\n\nThe Han faced widespread uprisings and the emergence of local warlords in the 100s and 200s CE.\nSinicized formerly-nomadic peoples who had settled in Northern China, such as the Xiongnu, rebelled and founded new dynasties.\nVarious Han Chinese dynasties in Southern China emerged in the 300s.\nDuring these conflicts, Buddhism was introduced to China via the Silk Road.\n\n#### Mid-imperial period\n\nChina was reunited under the Sui dynasty in 589, which constructed the Grand Canal to link Northern and Southern China.","source_title":"China","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_decision","ts_unix_ms":1791215836875}
