{"details":{"initial_reason":"object terms are not sufficiently present in the evidence span","model_output_excerpt":"{\n  \"claim\": {\n    \"subject\": \"Historical materialism\",\n    \"relation\": \"development\",\n    \"object\": \"theory\",\n    \"evidence_text\": \"Historical materialism was formulated by Karl Marx as a radical break with the prevailing philosophical and historical traditions of his time, synthesizing elements from German idealism, French socialist thought, and British political economy.\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.98,\n    \"question_relevance\": \"direct\",\n    \"valid_from\": null,\n    \"valid_until\": null\n  }\n}","question":"What is the development of Historical development?","reason":"object terms are not sufficiently present in the evidence span","selected_passage_excerpt":"Section: Later developments\n\n## Later developments\n\n### Second International\n\nKarl Kautsky\nGeorgi Plekhanov\n\nThe Second International (1889–1914) became the \"undoubted custodian of Marxist 'orthodoxy'\".\nThe interpretation of historical materialism developed during this period has been heavily criticized as a form of \"fatalistic\" and \"mechanically deterministic\" social evolutionism.\nIn his earlier work, however, Kautsky developed a more nuanced analysis that rejected unilinear models of history and stressed the causal significance of ideas.\n\nOther leading theorists of the era, such as Georgi Plekhanov and Antonio Labriola, sought to develop more sophisticated versions of historical materialism.\nPlekhanov praised Labriola's \"synthetic view of social life\" as a powerful alternative to both crude economism and ahistorical pluralism.\n\n### Russian Revolution and the Comintern\n\nVladimir Lenin\nLeon Trotsky\n\nThe Russian Revolution of 1917 and the subsequent formation of the Communist International (Comintern) marked a new stage in the development of historical materialism, with the revolutionary context spurring new theoretical work.\nVladimir Lenin, for example, summarized historical materialism with a long quotation from the 1859 Preface, arguing that Marx's greatness lay in showing how \"out of one system of social life another and higher system develops\", such as capitalism out of feudalism, \"in consequence of the growth of the productive forces\".\n\nA significant development of this period was Leon Trotsky's theory of combined and uneven development.\nBuilding on the insights of earlier theorists, Trotsky argued that capitalism's global expansion did not create a uniform path of development.\nThis process codified historical materialism into a rigid, unilinear, and fatalistic schema, which held that all societies must mechanically pass through a fixed sequence of stages: primitive communism, slave, feudal, capitalist, and socialist.\nHistorian Robert Brenner challenged Cohen's transhistorical \"Development Thesis,\" arguing that only specifically capitalist property relations created a systemic imperative for intensive economic growth.\n\nReacting to the perceived weaknesses of both Althusserian structuralism and Thompsonian voluntarism, thinkers like Alex Callinicos and the early Alasdair MacIntyre sought to develop a version of historical materialism that could synthesize structure and agency without reducing one to the other.\n\nSection: Origins and development\n\n## Orig","source_title":"Historical materialism","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_repair","ts_unix_ms":1791215886444}
{"details":{"initial_reason":"object terms are not sufficiently present in the evidence span","initial_status":"ungrounded","model_output_excerpt":"{\n  \"claim\": {\n    \"subject\": \"Historical materialism\",\n    \"relation\": \"development\",\n    \"object\": \"theory\",\n    \"evidence_text\": \"Historical materialism was formulated by Karl Marx as a radical break with the prevailing philosophical and historical traditions of his time, synthesizing elements from German idealism, French socialist thought, and British political economy.\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.98,\n    \"question_relevance\": \"direct\",\n    \"valid_from\": null,\n    \"valid_until\": null\n  }\n}","question":"What is the development of Historical development?","reason":"object terms are not sufficiently present in the evidence span","relevance":"direct","selected_passage_excerpt":"Section: Later developments\n\n## Later developments\n\n### Second International\n\nKarl Kautsky\nGeorgi Plekhanov\n\nThe Second International (1889–1914) became the \"undoubted custodian of Marxist 'orthodoxy'\".\nThe interpretation of historical materialism developed during this period has been heavily criticized as a form of \"fatalistic\" and \"mechanically deterministic\" social evolutionism.\nIn his earlier work, however, Kautsky developed a more nuanced analysis that rejected unilinear models of history and stressed the causal significance of ideas.\n\nOther leading theorists of the era, such as Georgi Plekhanov and Antonio Labriola, sought to develop more sophisticated versions of historical materialism.\nPlekhanov praised Labriola's \"synthetic view of social life\" as a powerful alternative to both crude economism and ahistorical pluralism.\n\n### Russian Revolution and the Comintern\n\nVladimir Lenin\nLeon Trotsky\n\nThe Russian Revolution of 1917 and the subsequent formation of the Communist International (Comintern) marked a new stage in the development of historical materialism, with the revolutionary context spurring new theoretical work.\nVladimir Lenin, for example, summarized historical materialism with a long quotation from the 1859 Preface, arguing that Marx's greatness lay in showing how \"out of one system of social life another and higher system develops\", such as capitalism out of feudalism, \"in consequence of the growth of the productive forces\".\n\nA significant development of this period was Leon Trotsky's theory of combined and uneven development.\nBuilding on the insights of earlier theorists, Trotsky argued that capitalism's global expansion did not create a uniform path of development.\nThis process codified historical materialism into a rigid, unilinear, and fatalistic schema, which held that all societies must mechanically pass through a fixed sequence of stages: primitive communism, slave, feudal, capitalist, and socialist.\nHistorian Robert Brenner challenged Cohen's transhistorical \"Development Thesis,\" arguing that only specifically capitalist property relations created a systemic imperative for intensive economic growth.\n\nReacting to the perceived weaknesses of both Althusserian structuralism and Thompsonian voluntarism, thinkers like Alex Callinicos and the early Alasdair MacIntyre sought to develop a version of historical materialism that could synthesize structure and agency without reducing one to the other.\n\nSection: Origins and development\n\n## Orig","source_title":"Historical materialism","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_decision","ts_unix_ms":1791215886447}
{"details":{"initial_reason":"subject, relation, or object lacks direct support in the cited words","model_output_excerpt":"{\"claim\":\"Historical development\",\"relation\":\"definition\",\"object\":\"modes of production\",\"evidence_text\":\"Historical development is understood as a succession of modes of production\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"question_relevance\":\"direct\",\"valid_from\":null,\"valid_until\":null}","question":"What is the development of Historical development?","reason":"subject, relation, or object lacks direct support in the cited words","selected_passage_excerpt":"Historical materialism is a theory of history and sociology in Marxist thought that posits that material and economic conditions are the primary drivers of societal structure and historical change.\nHistorical development is understood as a succession of modes of production, with transitions between them driven by contradictions that arise between the developing forces of production (technology, labour power) and the static relations of production (class structure, property ownership).\nThe materialist conception of history was developed by Marx as a critical response to German idealist philosophy, the ahistorical materialism of the Enlightenment, and the classical political economy of thinkers like Adam Smith and David Ricardo.","source_title":"Historical materialism","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_repair","ts_unix_ms":1791215888600}
{"details":{"initial_reason":"subject, relation, or object lacks direct support in the cited words","initial_status":"ungrounded","model_output_excerpt":"{\"claim\":\"Historical development\",\"relation\":\"definition\",\"object\":\"modes of production\",\"evidence_text\":\"Historical development is understood as a succession of modes of production\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"question_relevance\":\"direct\",\"valid_from\":null,\"valid_until\":null}","question":"What is the development of Historical development?","reason":"subject, relation, or object lacks direct support in the cited words","relevance":"direct","selected_passage_excerpt":"Historical materialism is a theory of history and sociology in Marxist thought that posits that material and economic conditions are the primary drivers of societal structure and historical change.\nHistorical development is understood as a succession of modes of production, with transitions between them driven by contradictions that arise between the developing forces of production (technology, labour power) and the static relations of production (class structure, property ownership).\nThe materialist conception of history was developed by Marx as a critical response to German idealist philosophy, the ahistorical materialism of the Enlightenment, and the classical political economy of thinkers like Adam Smith and David Ricardo.","source_title":"Historical materialism","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_decision","ts_unix_ms":1791215888601}
{"details":{"initial_reason":"object adds no factual detail beyond the subject","model_output_excerpt":"{\"claim\": \"The historical roots of papal primacy can be traced back to the early centuries of Christianity\", \"relation\": \"development\", \"object\": \"papal primacy\", \"evidence_text\": \"The historical roots of papal primacy can be traced back to the early centuries of Christianity\", \"confidence\": 0.98, \"question_relevance\": \"direct\", \"valid_from\": null, \"valid_until\": null}","question":"What is the development of Historical development?","reason":"object adds no factual detail beyond the subject","selected_passage_excerpt":"The historical roots of papal primacy can be traced back to the early centuries of Christianity, wherein the bishop of Rome, commonly referred to as the Pope, gradually accrued increasing authority and recognition.\nA confluence of historical, theological, and political factors contributed to this development.\nSome earliest references to the primacy of the bishop of Rome can be found in the writings of renowned Christian figures such as Ignatius of Antioch and Irenaeus of Lyon.","source_title":"History of papal primacy","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_repair","ts_unix_ms":1791215891919}
