ajor contributor of economic value to local communities.\nLegal protection of cultural property comprises a number of international...","source_title":"Cultural heritage","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_decision","ts_unix_ms":1791215938062}
{"details":{"initial_reason":"subject terms are not sufficiently present in the evidence span","model_output_excerpt":"{\n  \"claim\": \"Intangible cultural heritage (ICH) includes customs, beliefs, traditions, folklore, language, and knowledge\",\n  \"relation\": \"comprises\",\n  \"object\": \"customs, beliefs, traditions, folklore, language, and knowledge\",\n  \"evidence_text\": \"cultural properties such as historic sites, monuments, and artifacts—comprises manifestations of intellectual wealth such as customs, beliefs, traditions, folklore, language, and knowledge\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.98,\n  \"question_relevance\": \"direct\",\n  \"valid_from\": null,\n  \"valid_until\": null\n}","question":"What is the heritage of Cultural heritage?","reason":"subject terms are not sufficiently present in the evidence span","selected_passage_excerpt":"Intangible cultural heritage (ICH)—as opposed to a place's tangible cultural heritage, cultural properties such as historic sites, monuments, and artifacts—comprises manifestations of intellectual wealth such as customs, beliefs, traditions, folklore, language, and knowledge, as expressed in particular through craftsmanship and performance.\nResponding to the perceived over-representation of Western Europe among World Heritage Sites, to help valorize cultural diversity, and following on from earlier related measures in Japan (1950) and in South Korea (1962), in 2001, UNESCO made a survey among states and NGOs to try to agree on a definition, and the Convention for the Safeguarding of the Intangible Cultural Heritage was drafted in 2003 for its protection and promotion.","source_title":"Intangible cultural heritage","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_repair","ts_unix_ms":1791215942767}
