{"details":{"initial_reason":"exact passage and claim term checks passed","initial_status":"accepted","model_output_excerpt":"{\"claim\":\"American political ideologies\",\"relation\":\"conventionally align with\",\"object\":\"the left–right political spectrum\",\"evidence_text\":\"American political ideologies conventionally align with the left–right political spectrum\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"question_relevance\":\"direct\",\"valid_from\":null,\"valid_until\":null}","question":"What is the part_of of political ideology?","reason":"exact passage and claim term checks passed","relevance":"direct","selected_passage_excerpt":"American political ideologies conventionally align with the left–right political spectrum, with most Americans identifying as conservative, liberal, or moderate.\nThe former ideology developed as a response to  communism and then the civil rights movement, while the latter developed as a response to the New Deal.\nHistorical political movements in the United States have been shaped by ideologies as varied as republicanism, populism, separatism, fascism, socialism, monarchism, and nationalism.\nPolitical ideology in the United States began with the country's formation during the American Revolution, when republicanism challenged the preexisting monarchism that had defined the colonial governments.","source_title":"Political ideologies in the United States","status":"accepted"},"event":"scholar.extraction_decision","ts_unix_ms":1791216254517}
