{"details":{"initial_reason":"subject terms are not sufficiently present in the evidence span","model_output_excerpt":"{\n  \"claim\": {\n    \"subject\": \"solar and wind energy\",\n    \"relation\": \"contribution to global efforts in combating climate change\",\n    \"object\": \"mitigation options\",\n    \"evidence_text\": \"The IPCC estimates that these two mitigation options have the largest potential to reduce emissions before 2030 at low cost.\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.98,\n    \"question_relevance\": \"direct\",\n    \"valid_from\": null,\n    \"valid_until\": null\n  }\n}","question":"How does the integration of solar and wind energy contribute to global efforts in combating climate change?","reason":"subject terms are not sufficiently present in the evidence span","selected_passage_excerpt":"Section: Solar and wind energy\n\n### Solar and wind energy\n\nMain articles: Solar energy and Wind power\nThe 150 MW Andasol solar power station is a commercial parabolic trough solar thermal power plant, located in Spain.\nThe Andasol plant uses tanks of molten salt to store solar energy so that it can continue generating electricity for 7.5 hours after the sun has stopped shining.\n\nWind and sun can provide large amounts of low-carbon energy at competitive production costs.\nThe IPCC estimates that these two mitigation options have the largest potential to reduce emissions before 2030 at low cost.\n\nSolar photovoltaics (PV) has become the lowest-cost source of electricity generation in many regions globally.\n\nA 2024 review found that the global levelized cost of electricity for solar PV had fallen to between US$0.039 and 0.041 per kWh.\nA different technology is concentrated solar power (CSP).\nWith CSP, the energy can be stored for a few hours.\nSolar water heating doubled between 2010 and 2019.\n\nThe Shepherds Flat Wind Farm is an 845 megawatt (MW) nameplate capacity, wind farm in the US state of Oregon.\nEach turbine is a nameplate 2 or 2.5 MW electricity generator.\n\nRegions in the higher northern and southern latitudes have the greatest potential for wind power.\nFor this reason, combinations of wind and solar power lead to better-balanced systems.\n\nSection: Avoided costs of climate change effects\n\n### Avoided costs of climate change effects\n\nSee also: Economic impacts of climate change\n\nIt is possible to avoid some of the costs of the effects of climate change by limiting climate change.\nAccording to the Stern Review, inaction can be as high as the equivalent of losing at least 5% of global gross domestic product (GDP) each year, now and forever.\nBut mitigating climate change will only cost about 2% of GDP.","source_title":"Climate change mitigation","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_repair","ts_unix_ms":1791216382895}
{"details":{"initial_reason":"subject terms are not sufficiently present in the evidence span","initial_status":"ungrounded","model_output_excerpt":"{\n  \"claim\": {\n    \"subject\": \"solar and wind energy\",\n    \"relation\": \"contribution to global efforts in combating climate change\",\n    \"object\": \"mitigation options\",\n    \"evidence_text\": \"The IPCC estimates that these two mitigation options have the largest potential to reduce emissions before 2030 at low cost.\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.98,\n    \"question_relevance\": \"direct\",\n    \"valid_from\": null,\n    \"valid_until\": null\n  }\n}","question":"How does the integration of solar and wind energy contribute to global efforts in combating climate change?","reason":"subject terms are not sufficiently present in the evidence span","relevance":"direct","selected_passage_excerpt":"Section: Solar and wind energy\n\n### Solar and wind energy\n\nMain articles: Solar energy and Wind power\nThe 150 MW Andasol solar power station is a commercial parabolic trough solar thermal power plant, located in Spain.\nThe Andasol plant uses tanks of molten salt to store solar energy so that it can continue generating electricity for 7.5 hours after the sun has stopped shining.\n\nWind and sun can provide large amounts of low-carbon energy at competitive production costs.\nThe IPCC estimates that these two mitigation options have the largest potential to reduce emissions before 2030 at low cost.\n\nSolar photovoltaics (PV) has become the lowest-cost source of electricity generation in many regions globally.\n\nA 2024 review found that the global levelized cost of electricity for solar PV had fallen to between US$0.039 and 0.041 per kWh.\nA different technology is concentrated solar power (CSP).\nWith CSP, the energy can be stored for a few hours.\nSolar water heating doubled between 2010 and 2019.\n\nThe Shepherds Flat Wind Farm is an 845 megawatt (MW) nameplate capacity, wind farm in the US state of Oregon.\nEach turbine is a nameplate 2 or 2.5 MW electricity generator.\n\nRegions in the higher northern and southern latitudes have the greatest potential for wind power.\nFor this reason, combinations of wind and solar power lead to better-balanced systems.\n\nSection: Avoided costs of climate change effects\n\n### Avoided costs of climate change effects\n\nSee also: Economic impacts of climate change\n\nIt is possible to avoid some of the costs of the effects of climate change by limiting climate change.\nAccording to the Stern Review, inaction can be as high as the equivalent of losing at least 5% of global gross domestic product (GDP) each year, now and forever.\nBut mitigating climate change will only cost about 2% of GDP.","source_title":"Climate change mitigation","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_decision","ts_unix_ms":1791216382896}
{"details":{"initial_reason":"subject terms are not sufficiently present in the evidence span","model_output_excerpt":"{\"claim\":{\"subject\":\"global efforts in combating climate change\",\"relation\":\"contribution to climate change mitigation\",\"object\":\"climate change mitigation\",\"evidence_text\":\"2022 assessments emphasize that global greenhouse gas emissions must peak before 2025 and decline by about 43% by 2030 to limit warming to 1.5 °C, requiring rapid transitions in energy, transport, and land-use systems.\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"question_relevance\":\"direct\",\"valid_from\":null,\"valid_until\":null}}","question":"How does the integration of solar and wind energy contribute to global efforts in combating climate change?","reason":"subject terms are not sufficiently present in the evidence span","selected_passage_excerpt":"Climate change mitigation, also called decarbonisation, is an action to limit the greenhouse gases in the atmosphere that cause climate change.\nClimate change mitigation actions include conserving energy and replacing fossil fuels with clean energy sources.\nSecondary mitigation strategies include changes to land use and removing carbon dioxide (CO2) from the atmosphere.\n2022 assessments emphasize that global greenhouse gas emissions must peak before 2025 and decline by about 43% by 2030 to limit warming to 1.5 °C, requiring rapid transitions in energy, transport, and land-use systems.\nThe adopted climate change mitigation policies are insufficient, as they contribute to some changes but fail to accelerate transitions at the scale and speed required, and would still result in global warming of about 2.7 °C by 2100, significantly above the 2015 Paris Agreement's goal of limiting global warming to below 2 °C.\nRecent research shows that demand-side climate solutions—such as shifts in transportation behavior, dietary change, improved building energy efficiency, and reduced material consumption—could reduce global greenhouse gas emissions by 40% to 70% by 2050 while improving human well-being...","source_title":"Climate change mitigation","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_repair","ts_unix_ms":1791216387752}
