es, challenging the traditional power structures of renewable energy projects.\nCommunity renewable energy projects, and the slew of positive social impacts often associated with them, are only successful with institutional support.\nScholarships highlights the prevalence of institutional support in the EU and the UK for community based renewable energy, and not surprisingly, there are many more successful projects in Europe than there are in the United States.\n\nExisting literature shows a clear lack of institutional support for community-based renewable energy projects within the United States.\nThe private sector has especially increased interest in community solar energy projects within the past couple of years.\nThe differing approaches to community-based renewable energy projects globally may be attributed to the differences within the liberal welfare states of Europe compared to the United States.\n\nSection: Renewable energy and carbon dioxide emissions\n\n## Renewable energy and carbon dioxide emissions\n\nBetween 2010 and 2020, the cost of wind, solar, and natural gas dropped dramatically.\nenergy-related carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions would decrease by 2.5% in 2019 and by 1.0% in 2020 due to a shift away from coal and toward renewables and natural gas.\n\nRenewable energy has the potential to reduce CO2 emissions in three key energy use sectors: transport, heating and co","source_title":"Renewable energy in the United States","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_repair","ts_unix_ms":1791217370913}
{"details":{"initial_reason":"object terms are not sufficiently present in the evidence span","initial_status":"ungrounded","model_output_excerpt":"```json\n{\n  \"claim\": {\n    \"subject\": \"Community renewable energy projects\",\n    \"relation\": \"is\",\n    \"object\": \"projects that prioritize the participation of the end-user communities\",\n    \"evidence_text\": \"Community renewable energy projects, and the slew of positive social impacts often associated with them, are only successful with institutional support.\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.98,\n    \"question_relevance\": \"direct\",\n    \"valid_from\": null,\n    \"valid_until\": null\n  }\n}\n```","question":"How does the adoption of renewable energy technologies affect food production and distribution in developing countries?","reason":"object terms are not sufficiently present in the evidence span","relevance":"direct","selected_passage_excerpt":"Section: Community renewable energy\n\n## Community renewable energy\n\nSee also: Community solar\n\nCommunity renewable energy efforts incorporate calls for energy democracy to place the responsibility of the planning and implementation processes of a renewable energy project within a community.\nCommunity based renewable energy projects are expected to have a wide range of positive social impacts on top of decreasing fossil fuel usage, such as \"acceptance of renewable energy developments; awareness of renewable and sustainable energy technologies and issues; uptake of low carbon technologies; and sustainable/pro-environmental behavior\".\nHowever, in a survey conducted within a community about community renewable energy projects, results showed that while there was widespread support for implementation, there were low levels of desire for personal involvement, meaning many people shied away from potentially serving as project leaders.\nOften times, the degree of ownership is debated within the planning process.\n\nCommunity renewable energy projects prioritize the participation of the end-user communities, challenging the traditional power structures of renewable energy projects.\nCommunity renewable energy projects, and the slew of positive social impacts often associated with them, are only successful with institutional support.\nScholarships highlights the prevalence of institutional support in the EU and the UK for community based renewable energy, and not surprisingly, there are many more successful projects in Europe than there are in the United States.\n\nExisting literature shows a clear lack of institutional support for community-based renewable energy projects within the United States.\nThe private sector has especially increased interest in community solar energy projects within the past couple of years.\nThe differing approaches to community-based renewable energy projects globally may be attributed to the differences within the liberal welfare states of Europe compared to the United States.\n\nSection: Renewable energy and carbon dioxide emissions\n\n## Renewable energy and carbon dioxide emissions\n\nBetween 2010 and 2020, the cost of wind, solar, and natural gas dropped dramatically.\nenergy-related carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions would decrease by 2.5% in 2019 and by 1.0% in 2020 due to a shift away from coal and toward renewables and natural gas.\n\nRenewable energy has the potential to reduce CO2 emissions in three key energy use sectors: transport, heating and co","source_title":"Renewable energy in the United States","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_decision","ts_unix_ms":1791217370914}
{"details":{"initial_reason":"evidence_text is not an exact substring of the selected passage","model_output_excerpt":"```json\n{\"claim\":{\"subject\":\"Renewable energy in the United States\",\"relation\":\"is\",\"object\":\"a source of renewable energy in the United States\",\"evidence_text\":\"According to data from the US Energy Information Administration, renewable energy accounted for 17.8% of total primary energy production and 22.7% of total utility-scale electricity generation in the United States in 2024, up from 8.4% and 21% in 2022. Since 2019, wind power has been the largest producer of renewable electricity in the country.\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"question_relevance\":\"direct\",\"valid_from\":null,\"valid_until\":null}}\n```","question":"How does the adoption of renewable energy technologies affect food production and distribution in developing countries?","reason":"evidence_text is not an exact substring of the selected passage","selected_passage_excerpt":"According to data from the US Energy Information Administration, renewable energy accounted for 17.8% of total primary energy production and 22.7% of total utility-scale electricity generation in the United States in 2024, up from 8.4% and 21% in 2022.\nSince 2019, wind power has been the largest producer of renewable electricity in the country.","source_title":"Renewable energy in the United States","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_repair","ts_unix_ms":1791217376237}
