{"details":{"initial_reason":"evidence_text is not an exact substring of the selected passage","model_output_excerpt":"```json\n{\n  \"claim\": {\n    \"subject\": \"renewable energy transition\",\n    \"relation\": \"is\",\n    \"object\": \"a term for energy transition\",\n    \"evidence_text\": \"Another term for energy transition is renewable energy transition.\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.98,\n    \"question_relevance\": \"direct\",\n    \"valid_from\": null,\n    \"valid_until\": null\n  }\n}\n```","question":"How does the adoption of renewable energy technologies affect food production and distribution in developing countries?","reason":"evidence_text is not an exact substring of the selected passage","selected_passage_excerpt":"An energy transition (or energy system transformation) is a major structural change to energy supply and consumption in an energy system.\nCurrently, a transition to sustainable energy is underway to limit climate change.\nMost of the sustainable energy is renewable energy.\nTherefore, another term for energy transition is renewable energy transition.\nThe current transition aims to reduce greenhouse gas emissions from energy quickly and sustainably, mostly by phasing-down fossil fuels and changing as many processes as possible to operate on low carbon electricity.\nA previous energy transition perhaps took place during the Industrial Revolution from 1760 onwards, from wood and other biomass to coal, followed by oil and later natural gas.\nOver three-quarters of the world's energy needs are met by burning fossil fuels, but this usage emits greenhouse gases.\nEnergy production and consumption are responsible for most human-caused greenhouse gas emissions.\nSince the late 2010s, the renewable energy transition has also been driven by the rapidly falling...","source_title":"Energy transition","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_repair","ts_unix_ms":1791217379838}
