{"details":{"initial_reason":"exact passage and claim term checks passed","initial_status":"accepted","model_output_excerpt":"```json\n{\n  \"claim\": {\n    \"subject\": \"Community energy systems\",\n    \"relation\": \"is\",\n    \"object\": \"integrated approach to using cogeneration, renewables and other technologies\",\n    \"evidence_text\": \"Sustainable community energy systems, pioneered by Woking Borough Council, provide an integrated approach to using cogeneration, renewables and other technologies to provide sustainable energy supplies to an urban community.\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.98,\n    \"question_relevance\": \"direct\",\n    \"valid_from\": null,\n    \"valid_until\": null\n  }\n}\n```","question":"How can renewable energy technologies be used to bridge the gap in healthcare access for underserved communities?","reason":"exact passage and claim term checks passed","relevance":"direct","selected_passage_excerpt":"Section: Community energy systems\n\n## Community energy systems\n\nSustainable community energy systems, pioneered by Woking Borough Council, provide an integrated approach to using cogeneration, renewables and other technologies to provide sustainable energy supplies to an urban community.\nIt is expected that the same approach will be developed in other towns and cities, including London.\nHighlands and Islands Community Energy Company based in Inverness are active in developing community-owned and led initiatives in Scotland.\n\nIn England, Low Carbon Hub, based in Oxford, has developed one of the country's largest portfolios of community-owned renewable energy, including Ray Valley Solar, one of the largest community-owned solar parks in the United Kingdom, and Sandford Hydro, the largest community-owned hydroelectric scheme on the River Thames.\nIn 2026, Low Carbon Hub announced plans to add the UK's first community-owned co-located battery energy storage system to Ray Valley Solar.\n\nAn energy positive house was built in Wales for £125,000 in July 2015.\n\nSection: Renewable heat\n\n## Renewable heat\n\nRenewable heat in the UK comes from biomass and biogas, direct renewable heat sources such as solar thermal and geothermal heat, and ambient heat captured by heat pumps.\n\nIn 2024, renewable heat demand was mainly solid biomass (60%) and heat pumps (27%).\nThey accounted for 27% of renewable heat demand in 2024.\nThe plant will produce 3 MW of renewable electricity.","source_title":"Renewable energy in the United Kingdom","status":"accepted"},"event":"scholar.extraction_decision","ts_unix_ms":1791217423310}
