{"details":{"initial_reason":"evidence_text is not an exact substring of the selected passage","model_output_excerpt":"{\"claim\":{\"subject\":\"pho\",\"relation\":\"is described as\",\"object\":\"an Annamese soup held in high esteem, made with beef, a veal bone, onions, a bay leaf, salt, and pepper, and a small teaspoon of nuoc-mam (fish sauce)\",\"evidence_text\":\"Possibly the earliest English-language reference to pho was in the book Recipes of All Nations, edited by Countess Morphy in 1935: In the book, pho is described as \\\"an Annamese soup held in high esteem ... made with beef, a veal bone, onions, a bay leaf, salt, and pepper, and a small teaspoon of nuoc-mam (fish sauce).\\\"\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"question_relevance\":\"direct\",\"valid_from\":null,\"valid_until\":null}}","question":"What is the historical origin of chicken soup in different cultural regions?","reason":"evidence_text is not an exact substring of the selected passage","review_guidance":null,"selected_passage_excerpt":"Section: Etymology and origins\n\n## Etymology and origins\n\n────────────────────────────────\nPho\n────────────────────────────────\nVietnamese name\n────────────────────────┬───────\nVietnamese alphabet   │phở\n────────────────────────┼───────\nChữ Nôm               │頗\n────────────────────────┴───────\n\nReviews of 19th and 20th-century Vietnamese literature have found that pho entered the mainstream sometime in the 1910s.\nA 1931 dictionary is the first to define phở as a soup: \"from the word phấn.\nA dish consisting of small slices of rice cake boiled with beef.\"\n\nPossibly the earliest English-language reference to pho was in the book Recipes of All Nations, edited by Countess Morphy in 1935: In the book, pho is described as \"an Annamese soup held in high esteem ...\nmade with beef, a veal bone, onions, a bay leaf, salt, and pepper, and a small teaspoon of nuoc-mam (fish sauce).\"\n\nThere are two prevailing theories on the origin of the word phở and, by extension, the dish itself.\nFrench settlers commonly ate beef, whereas Vietnamese traditionally ate pork and chicken and used cattle primarily as beasts of burden.\nAnother suggestion of a separate origin is that phở in French has long been pronounced fo rather than fø: in Jean Tardieu's Lettre de Hanoï à Roger Martin Du Gard (1928), a soup vendor cries \"Pho-ô!\" in the street.\n\nMany Hanoians explain that the word phở derives from French soldiers' ordering \"feu\" (fire) from gánh phở, referring to both the steam rising from a bowl of phở and the wood fire seen glowing from a gánh phở in the evening.\n\nFood historian Erica J.\nPeters argues that the French has embraced phở in a way that overlooks its origins as a local improvisation, reinforcing \"an idea that the French brought modern ingenuity to a traditionalist Vietnam\".\nThe connection between phở and the French culinary tradition remains widely debated but remains a prominent theory in discussions of its origins.\n\n### From Cantonese\n\nAnother possible origin links phở to Chinese influences.","source_title":"Pho","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_repair","ts_unix_ms":1791225582402}
{"details":{"initial_reason":"evidence_text is not an exact substring of the selected passage","initial_status":"ungrounded","model_output_excerpt":"{\"claim\":{\"subject\":\"pho\",\"relation\":\"is described as\",\"object\":\"an Annamese soup held in high esteem, made with beef, a veal bone, onions, a bay leaf, salt, and pepper, and a small teaspoon of nuoc-mam (fish sauce)\",\"evidence_text\":\"Possibly the earliest English-language reference to pho was in the book Recipes of All Nations, edited by Countess Morphy in 1935: In the book, pho is described as \\\"an Annamese soup held in high esteem ... made with beef, a veal bone, onions, a bay leaf, salt, and pepper, and a small teaspoon of nuoc-mam (fish sauce).\\\"\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"question_relevance\":\"direct\",\"valid_from\":null,\"valid_until\":null}}","question":"What is the historical origin of chicken soup in different cultural regions?","reason":"evidence_text is not an exact substring of the selected passage","relevance":"direct","review_guidance":null,"selected_passage_excerpt":"Section: Etymology and origins\n\n## Etymology and origins\n\n────────────────────────────────\nPho\n────────────────────────────────\nVietnamese name\n────────────────────────┬───────\nVietnamese alphabet   │phở\n────────────────────────┼───────\nChữ Nôm               │頗\n────────────────────────┴───────\n\nReviews of 19th and 20th-century Vietnamese literature have found that pho entered the mainstream sometime in the 1910s.\nA 1931 dictionary is the first to define phở as a soup: \"from the word phấn.\nA dish consisting of small slices of rice cake boiled with beef.\"\n\nPossibly the earliest English-language reference to pho was in the book Recipes of All Nations, edited by Countess Morphy in 1935: In the book, pho is described as \"an Annamese soup held in high esteem ...\nmade with beef, a veal bone, onions, a bay leaf, salt, and pepper, and a small teaspoon of nuoc-mam (fish sauce).\"\n\nThere are two prevailing theories on the origin of the word phở and, by extension, the dish itself.\nFrench settlers commonly ate beef, whereas Vietnamese traditionally ate pork and chicken and used cattle primarily as beasts of burden.\nAnother suggestion of a separate origin is that phở in French has long been pronounced fo rather than fø: in Jean Tardieu's Lettre de Hanoï à Roger Martin Du Gard (1928), a soup vendor cries \"Pho-ô!\" in the street.\n\nMany Hanoians explain that the word phở derives from French soldiers' ordering \"feu\" (fire) from gánh phở, referring to both the steam rising from a bowl of phở and the wood fire seen glowing from a gánh phở in the evening.\n\nFood historian Erica J.\nPeters argues that the French has embraced phở in a way that overlooks its origins as a local improvisation, reinforcing \"an idea that the French brought modern ingenuity to a traditionalist Vietnam\".\nThe connection between phở and the French culinary tradition remains widely debated but remains a prominent theory in discussions of its origins.\n\n### From Cantonese\n\nAnother possible origin links phở to Chinese influences.","source_title":"Pho","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_decision","ts_unix_ms":1791225582404}
{"details":{"initial_reason":"exact passage and claim term checks passed","initial_status":"accepted","model_output_excerpt":"{\"claim\":{\"subject\":\"pho\",\"relation\":\"is a\",\"object\":\"Vietnamese soup dish consisting of broth, rice noodles (bánh phở), herbs, and meat – usually beef (phở bò), and sometimes chicken (phở gà)\",\"evidence_text\":\"Pho (Vietnamese: phở [fɤ˧˩˧] ) is a Vietnamese soup dish consisting of broth, rice noodles (bánh phở), herbs, and meat – usually beef (phở bò), and sometimes chicken (phở gà).\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"question_relevance\":\"direct\",\"valid_from\":null,\"valid_until\":null}}","question":"What is the historical origin of chicken soup in different cultural regions?","reason":"exact passage and claim term checks passed","relevance":"direct","review_guidance":null,"selected_passage_excerpt":"Pho (Vietnamese: phở [fɤ˧˩˧] ) is a Vietnamese soup dish consisting of broth, rice noodles (bánh phở), herbs, and meat – usually beef (phở bò), and sometimes chicken (phở gà).\nDue to limited historical documentation, the origins of pho remain debated.\nThe Hanoi (northern) and Saigon (southern) styles of pho differ by noodle width, sweetness of broth, and choice of herbs and sauce.\nIn 2017, Vietnam made December 12 the \"Day of Pho\" (Ngày của Phở).","source_title":"Pho","status":"accepted"},"event":"scholar.extraction_decision","ts_unix_ms":1791225594596}
