{"details":{"evidence_match":null,"initial_reason":"scholar returned invalid JSON: missing field `evidence_text` at line 1 column 152","initial_status":"invalid_json","model_output_excerpt":"{\"claim\":{\"subject\":\"The Oxford English Dictionary\",\"relation\":\"defines\",\"object\":\"A soup made with potatoes, leeks, and cream, usually served chilled\"},\"evidence_text\":\"The Oxford English Dictionary defines the former as \"A soup made with potatoes, leeks, and cream, usually served chilled\", and the latter as \"A cold Spanish vegetable soup consisting of onions, cucumbers, pimentos, etc., chopped very small with bread and put into a bowl of oil, vinegar, and water\".\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"question_relevance\":\"direct\",\"valid_from\":null,\"valid_until\":null}","question":"What is the typical preparation time for traditional chicken soup?","reason":"scholar returned invalid JSON: missing field `evidence_text` at line 1 column 152","relevance":"unknown","review_guidance":"Extract the sentence about preparation time from the 'Preparation' section.","selected_passage_chars":1997,"selected_passage_excerpt":"Section: Ancient times and later\n\n### Ancient times and later\n\nIn 1988 the food writer M.\nFisher commented, \"It is impossible to think of any good meal, no matter how plain or elegant, without soup or bread in it.\nMethods of making soup evolved from one culture to another.\nThe first soups were made from grains and herbs; later, peas, beans, other vegetables, pasta, meat or fish were added.\nIn her 2010 work Soup: A Global History, Janet Clarkson writes that the ancient Romans had a great variety of soups.\nClarkson comments that about a fifth of May's recipes are for soups of one kind or another.\n\nThe Huangdi Neijing, a Chinese medicinal text, describes the preparation of soups and clear liquids by steaming rice, and recommends soups as medicine.\n\nIn the eighteenth century, meals at grand European tables were still served in the style that had persisted since the Middle Ages, with successive courses of three or four dishes placed on the table simultaneously and then replaced by three or more contrasting dishes.\nSoup was typically part of the first course.\nIn the early nineteenth century a new style of dining became fashionable in Europe and elsewhere: service à la russe – Russian-style service: dishes were served one at a time, usually beginning with soup.\n\nCite error: There are `<ref group=n>` tags on this page, but the references will not show without a `{{reflist|group=n}}` template (see the help page).\n\nSection: Cold soups\n\n## Cold soups\n\nbowl of brightly coloured vegetable soupGazpacho\nSee also: List of cold soups\n\nCold soups are a particular variation on the traditional soup.\nTwo well-known chilled soups are the Franco-American vichyssoise and the Spanish gazpacho.\nThe Oxford English Dictionary defines the former as \"A soup made with potatoes, leeks, and cream, usually served chilled\", and the latter as \"A cold Spanish vegetable soup consisting of onions, cucumbers, pimentos, etc., chopped very small with bread and put into a bowl of oil, vinegar, and water\".","selected_passage_truncated":false,"semantic_support":null,"source_title":"Soup","status":"invalid_json"},"event":"scholar.extraction_decision","ts_unix_ms":1791231378340}
