{"details":{"evidence_match":{"method":"exact","original_passage_span":"The name beryllium for the element was first used by Wöhler upon its isolation.","passage_byte_start":7353,"proposed_evidence":"The name beryllium for the element was first used by Wöhler upon its isolation.","proposed_evidence_chars":79},"initial_reason":"AI semantic entailment confirmed against original source quotation","initial_status":"accepted","model_output_excerpt":"{\n  \"claim\": {\n    \"subject\": \"Fluorine\",\n    \"relation\": \"was first used by\",\n    \"object\": \"Wöhler\",\n    \"evidence_text\": \"The name beryllium for the element was first used by Wöhler upon its isolation.\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.98,\n    \"question_relevance\": \"direct\",\n    \"valid_from\": null,\n    \"valid_until\": null\n  }\n}","question":"Which element was the first to be discovered and named by a specific scientist, and what was the original name given to it before its modern designation?","reason":"AI semantic entailment confirmed against original source quotation","relevance":"direct","review_guidance":"Find a source that directly addresses the question about the first discovered and named element and its original name.","selected_passage_chars":8365,"selected_passage_excerpt":"Section: Modern discoveries\n\nSince the true nature of those compounds was sometimes only gradually discovered, it is sometimes very difficult to name one specific discoverer.\nThe name was proposed to be changed to silicon by Thomas Thomson in 1817, and this was eventually accepted because of its analogies to boron and carbon.\n───┼──────────────────┼─────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n13 │Aluminium       │1746     │J.\nGiven its present name in 1779 by Guyton de Morveau; prior to that it was called magnesia.\n───┼──────────────────┼─────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n9  │Fluorine        │1771     │W.\nBussy                               │Vauquelin discovered the oxide in beryl and emerald in 1798, and was uncertain about the name to give to it: in 1798 he called it la terre du beril, but the journal editors named it glucine after the sweet taste of beryllium compounds (which are highly toxic).\nThe name beryllium for the element was first used by Wöhler upon its isolation.\nde Marignac                                     │Ekeberg found another element in minerals similar to columbite, and named it after Tantalus from Greek mythology because of its inability to be dissolved by acids (just as Tantalus was tantalised by water that receded when he tried to drink it).\nNorton                      │Hisinger and Berzelius discovered a new earth in cerite, considered it to be an oxide of a new element and named the element after the newly discovered asteroid (then considered a planet), Ceres.\nTennant                                               │Tennant had been working on samples of South American platinum in parallel with Wollaston and discovered two new elements, which he named osmium and iridium, and published the iridium results in 1804.\n\nSection: Periodic table of elements\n\n## Periodic table of elements\n\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────","selected_passage_truncated":true,"semantic_support":{"evidence_text":"The name beryllium for the element was first used by Wöhler upon its isolation.","model_verdict":"entailed","object":"Wöhler","reason":