{"details":{"evidence_match":{"method":"exact","original_passage_span":"Some old, high-powered lamps used in theater, projection, searchlight, and lighthouse service with heavy, sturdy filaments contained loose tungsten powder within the envelope.","passage_byte_start":728,"proposed_evidence":"Some old, high-powered lamps used in theater, projection, searchlight, and lighthouse service with heavy, sturdy filaments contained loose tungsten powder within the envelope.","proposed_evidence_chars":175},"initial_reason":"AI semantic entailment confirmed against original source quotation","initial_status":"accepted","model_output_excerpt":"{\n  \"claim\": {\n    \"subject\": \"tungsten powder\",\n    \"relation\": \"contained within\",\n    \"object\": \"light bulb envelope\",\n    \"evidence_text\": \"Some old, high-powered lamps used in theater, projection, searchlight, and lighthouse service with heavy, sturdy filaments contained loose tungsten powder within the envelope.\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.98,\n    \"question_relevance\": \"direct\",\n    \"valid_from\": null,\n    \"valid_until\": null\n  }\n}","question":"Who is credited with the first successful demonstration of a practical incandescent light bulb in 1879?","reason":"AI semantic entailment confirmed against original source quotation","relevance":"direct","review_guidance":null,"selected_passage_chars":2826,"selected_passage_excerpt":"Section: Bulb blackening\n\n### Bulb blackening\n\nIn a conventional lamp, the evaporated tungsten condenses on the inner surface of the glass envelope, darkening it.\nFor bulbs that contain a vacuum, the darkening is uniform across the entire surface of the envelope.\nStudy of the problem of bulb blackening led to the discovery of thermionic emission, the invention of the vacuum tube, and evaporation deposition used to make mirrors and other optical coatings.\n\nA very small amount of water vapor inside a light bulb can significantly increase lamp darkening.\nSmall amounts of substances such as zirconium are placed within the lamp as a getter to react with any oxygen that may bake out of the lamp components during operation.\n\nSome old, high-powered lamps used in theater, projection, searchlight, and lighthouse service with heavy, sturdy filaments contained loose tungsten powder within the envelope.\nFrom time to time, the operator would remove the bulb and shake it, allowing the tungsten powder to scrub off most of the tungsten that had condensed on the interior of the envelope, removing the blackening and brightening the lamp again.\n\nSection: Bulb shapes\n\n### Bulb shapes\n\nIncandescent light bulbs come in a range of shapes and sizes.\n\nBulb shape and size designations are given in national standards.\nA55 or PAR38, where the letters identify the shape and the numbers some characteristic size.\n\nNational standards such as ANSI C79.1-2002, IS 14897:2000 and JIS C 7710:1988 cover a common terminology for bulb shapes.\n\n────────────────────────┬──────────┬──────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nDescription             │SI        │Inch      │Details\n────────────────────────┼──────────┼──────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\"Standard\" lightbulb    │A60 E26   │A19 E26   │⌀60 mm (~⌀19/8 in) A series bulb, ⌀26 mm Edison screw\n────────────────────────┼──────────┼──────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nCandle-flame bulb       │CA35 E12  │CA11 E12  │⌀35 mm (~⌀11/8 in) candle-flame shape, ⌀12 mm Edison screw\n────────────────────────┼──────────┼──────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nFlood light             │BR95 E26  │BR30 E26  │⌀95 mm (~⌀30/8 in) flood light, ⌀26 mm Edison screw\n────────────────────────┼──────────┼──────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nHalogen track-light bulb│MR50 GU5.3│MR16 GU5.3│⌀50 mm (~⌀16/8 in) multifaceted reflector, 5.33 mm-spaced 12 V bi-pin connector\n────────────────────────┴──────────┴──────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────","selected_passage_truncated":false,"semantic_support":{"evidence_text":"Some old, high-powered lamps used in theater, projection, searchlight, and lighthouse service with heavy, sturdy filaments contained loose tungsten powder within the envelope.","model_verdict":"entailed","object":"light bulb envelope","reason":"The source explicitly states that loose tungsten powder was contained within the envelope of some old high-powered lamps.","relation":"contained within","stance_override":null,"subject":"tungsten powder","valid_from":null,"valid_until":null,"verdict":"entailed"},"source_title":"Incandescent light bulb","status":"accepted"},"event":"scholar.extraction_decision","ts_unix_ms":1791231840841}
