{"details":{"evidence_match":{"method":"exact","original_passage_span":"In 1878, Edison began working on a system of electrical illumination that he could deploy in a large-scale commercial utility, something he hoped could compete with gas and oil-based lighting.","passage_byte_start":359,"proposed_evidence":"In 1878, Edison began working on a system of electrical illumination that he could deploy in a large-scale commercial utility, something he hoped could compete with gas and oil-based lighting.","proposed_evidence_chars":192},"initial_reason":"claim validity dates are invalid or absent from the passage","model_output_excerpt":"{\n  \"claim\": {\n    \"subject\": \"Edison\",\n    \"relation\": \"began working on\",\n    \"object\": \"a system of electrical illumination\",\n    \"evidence_text\": \"In 1878, Edison began working on a system of electrical illumination that he could deploy in a large-scale commercial utility, something he hoped could compete with gas and oil-based lighting.\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.98,\n    \"question_relevance\": \"direct\",\n    \"valid_from\": \"1878\",\n    \"valid_until\": \"1879\"\n  }\n}","question":"Who is credited with the first successful demonstration of a practical incandescent light bulb in 1879?","reason":"claim validity dates are invalid or absent from the passage","review_guidance":"Find a source that directly addresses the original research question regarding the 1879 demonstration of the incandescent light bulb.","selected_passage_chars":2002,"selected_passage_excerpt":"Section: Electric light\n\n## Electric light\n\nFurther information: Incandescent light bulb\nThe inside of a museum exhibit shows a heavily used light-bulb with smoky glass.\nThere is a handwritten inscription on a label wrapped around the stem of the bulb.Edison's first successful model of light bulb, used in public demonstrations at Menlo Park, December 1879\n\nIn 1878, Edison began working on a system of electrical illumination that he could deploy in a large-scale commercial utility, something he hoped could compete with gas and oil-based lighting.\nMany incandescent lamps had been devised by inventors prior to Edison, but these early bulbs all had flaws that made them difficult to use on a large scale commercially.\nEdison first tried using a filament made of cardboard, carbonized with compressed lampblack, but this burnt out too quickly to provide lasting light.\nMorgan & Co., members of the Vanderbilt family, and other wealthy and connected investors he met through Grosvenor Lowrey.\n\nA diagram of a light bulb with Edison's name written above it the patent number and date are labeled.U.S.\nThe ship has two masts and a smokestack which is bellowing.The Oregon Railroad and Navigation Company's new steamship, the Columbia, was the first commercial application for Edison's incandescent light bulb in 1880.\n\nHenry Villard financed the construction of an electrically powered and lit train built on a custom track built by Edison's company.\nThe train worked and some of the technology was patented, but Edison elected to focus on the bulbs and did not follow through with developing the train.\n\nThe incandescent light bulb patented by Edison began to gain widespread popularity in Europe as well.\nIt employed a large workforce, which included up and coming engineers such as Reginald Fessenden and Nikola Tesla.\n\nAfter devising a commercially viable electric light bulb on October 21, 1879, Edison was determined to create an electric utility to compete with the existing gas light utilities.","selected_passage_truncated":false,"semantic_support":null,"source_title":"Thomas Edison","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_repair","ts_unix_ms":1791231880684}
{"details":{"evidence_match":{"method":"exact","original_passage_span":"In 1878, Edison began working on a system of electrical illumination that he could deploy in a large-scale commercial utility, something he hoped could compete with gas and oil-based lighting.","passage_byte_start":359,"proposed_evidence":"In 1878, Edison began working on a system of electrical illumination that he could deploy in a large-scale commercial utility, something he hoped could compete with gas and oil-based lighting.","proposed_evidence_chars":192},"initial_reason":"claim validity dates are invalid or absent from the passage","initial_status":"ungrounded","model_output_excerpt":"{\n  \"claim\": {\n    \"subject\": \"Edison\",\n    \"relation\": \"began working on\",\n    \"object\": \"a system of electrical illumination\",\n    \"evidence_text\": \"In 1878, Edison began working on a system of electrical illumination that he could deploy in a large-scale commercial utility, something he hoped could compete with gas and oil-based lighting.\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.98,\n    \"question_relevance\": \"direct\",\n    \"valid_from\": \"1878\",\n    \"valid_until\": \"1879\"\n  }\n}","question":"Who is credited with the first successful demonstration of a practical incandescent light bulb in 1879?","reason":"claim validity dates are invalid or absent from the passage","relevance":"direct","review_guidance":"Find a source that directly addresses the original research question regarding the 1879 demonstration of the incandescent light bulb.","selected_passage_chars":2002,"selected_passage_excerpt":"Section: Electric light\n\n## Electric light\n\nFurther information: Incandescent light bulb\nThe inside of a museum exhibit shows a heavily used light-bulb with smoky glass.\nThere is a handwritten inscription on a label wrapped around the stem of the bulb.Edison's first successful model of light bulb, used in public demonstrations at Menlo Park, December 1879\n\nIn 1878, Edison began working on a system of electrical illumination that he could deploy in a large-scale commercial utility, something he hoped could compete with gas and oil-based lighting.\nMany incandescent lamps had been devised by inventors prior to Edison, but these early bulbs all had flaws that made them difficult to use on a large scale commercially.\nEdison first tried using a filament made of cardboard, carbonized with compressed lampblack, but this burnt out too quickly to provide lasting light.\nMorgan & Co., members of the Vanderbilt family, and other wealthy and connected investors he met through Grosvenor Lowrey.\n\nA diagram of a light bulb with Edison's name written above it the patent number and date are labeled.U.S.\nThe ship has two masts and a smokestack which is bellowing.The Oregon Railroad and Navigation Company's new steamship, the Columbia, was the first commercial application for Edison's incandescent light bulb in 1880.\n\nHenry Villard financed the construction of an electrically powered and lit train built on a custom track built by Edison's company.\nThe train worked and some of the technology was patented, but Edison elected to focus on the bulbs and did not follow through with developing the train.\n\nThe incandescent light bulb patented by Edison began to gain widespread popularity in Europe as well.\nIt employed a large workforce, which included up and coming engineers such as Reginald Fessenden and Nikola Tesla.\n\nAfter devising a commercially viable electric light bulb on October 21, 1879, Edison was determined to create an electric utility to compete with the existing gas light utilities.","selected_passage_truncated":false,"semantic_support":null,"source_title":"Thomas Edison","status":"ungrounded"},"event":"scholar.extraction_decision","ts_unix_ms":1791231880685}
