{"details":{"evidence_match":{"method":"exact","original_passage_span":"Copper occurs naturally as native metallic copper and was known to some of the oldest civilizations on record.","passage_byte_start":2391,"proposed_evidence":"Copper occurs naturally as native metallic copper and was known to some of the oldest civilizations on record.","proposed_evidence_chars":110},"initial_reason":"AI semantic entailment confirmed against original source quotation","initial_status":"accepted","model_output_excerpt":"{\n  \"claim\": {\n    \"subject\": \"Copper\",\n    \"relation\": \"was known to\",\n    \"object\": \"some of the oldest civilizations on record\",\n    \"evidence_text\": \"Copper occurs naturally as native metallic copper and was known to some of the oldest civilizations on record.\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.98,\n    \"question_relevance\": \"direct\",\n    \"valid_from\": null,\n    \"valid_until\": null\n  }\n}","question":"Find reliable evidence that supports, contradicts, or qualifies this assertion: Ancient humans discovered copper as a natural resource.","reason":"AI semantic entailment confirmed against original source quotation","relevance":"direct","review_guidance":"No further action needed as the claim is directly supported.\nPrevious rejected claim: Copper was known to some of the oldest civilizations on record\nPreferred exact evidence from reviewer: none","selected_passage_chars":3955,"selected_passage_excerpt":"Section: Ancient and post-classical\n\n### Ancient and post-classical\n\nIn alchemy the symbol for copper was also the symbol for the goddess and planet Venus.\nIt was an important resource for the Romans, Greeks and other ancient peoples.\nIn Roman times, it was known as aes Cyprium, aes being the generic Latin term for copper alloys and Cyprium from Cyprus, where much copper was mined.\nAphrodite (Venus in Rome) represented copper in mythology and alchemy because of its lustrous beauty and its ancient use in producing mirrors; Cyprus, the source of copper, was sacred to the goddess.\nThe seven heavenly bodies known to the ancients were associated with the seven metals known in antiquity, and Venus was assigned to copper, both because of the connection to the goddess and because Venus was the brightest heavenly body after the Sun and Moon and so corresponded to the most lustrous and desirable metal after gold and silver.\n\nCopper was the most extensively used metal among natives of North America, with evidence for use going back 7000 years.\nThere are several exquisite copper plates, known as the Mississippian copper plates, that have been found in North America in the area around Cahokia, dating from this time period (1000–1300 AD).\n\nMississippian copper plates from North America were produced in this style from around 800 to 1600 AD.\n\nIn South America, a copper mask dated to 1000 BC found in the Argentinian Andes is the oldest known copper artifact discovered in the Andes.\nCopper metallurgy in Peru dates to around 500 BC, with larger scale production beginning around 900 AD as part of the rise of the Sican culture in northern Peru.\nThe production continued through a series of conquests by the Chimor and Inca cultures, ending with the Spanish conquest in 1532.\n\nIn Sub-Saharan Africa, throughout the period from the first mines around 2000 BC in Agades until the early 1800s, copper was viewed as more precious and prestigious than either gold or silver.\n\nSection: Copper Age\n\n#### Copper Age\n\nMain article: Copper Age\nAncient copper ingot\nA corroded copper ingot from Zakros, Crete, shaped in the form of an animal skin (oxhide) typical in that era\nCopper ore\nCopper ore (chrysocolla) in Cambrian sandstone from Chalcolithic mines in the Timna Valley, southern Israel\nCopper artifacts from the Old Copper Complex of North America, ~9500–5400 years before present\n\nCopper occurs naturally as native metallic copper and was known to some of the oldest civilizations on record.\nEvidence suggests that gold and native meteoric iron were the only metals used by humans before copper and hot metallurgy.\nThe history of copper metallurgy is thought to follow this sequence: first came cold working of native copper, then annealing, followed by smelting, and finally casting.^{citation needed}\n\nThe earliest archaeological support of hot metallurgy in Eurasia is found in the Balkans and Carpathian Mountains as evidenced by findings of copper objects made by metal casting and smelting dated to between 6200 and 5000 BC.\nÖtzi the Iceman, a male dated from 3300 to 3200 BC discovered in the Central Eastern Alps, was found with a 99.7% pure copper axhead; high levels of arsenic in his hair suggest an involvement in copper smelting.\nNative Copper usage by the indigenous peoples of the Old Copper Complex from the Great Lakes region of North America has been radiometrically dated to as far back as 7500 BC.\nIndigenous peoples of North America around the Great Lakes may have found 99% pure native copper veins during this time, making it one of the oldest known examples of copper extraction in the world.\nThere is evidence from prehistoric lead pollution from lakes in Michigan that people in the region began mining copper c.\nLater evidence suggests that utilitarian copper objects fell increasingly out of use in the Old Copper Complex of North America and a shift towards an increased production of ornamental copper objects occurred.","selected_passage_truncated":false,"semantic_support":{"evidence_text":"Copper occurs naturally as native metallic copper and was known to some of the oldest civilizations on record.","model_verdict":"entailed","object":"some of the oldest civilizations on record","reason":"The source explicitly states that copper was known to some of the oldest civilizations on record.","relation":"was known to","stance_override":null,"subject":"Copper","valid_from":null,"valid_until":null,"verdict":"entailed"},"source_title":"Copper","status":"accepted"},"event":"scholar.extraction_decision","ts_unix_ms":1791233061890}
