{"details":{"evidence_match":{"method":"exact","original_passage_span":"These kinds of query strings are very similar to cookies in that both contain arbitrary pieces of information chosen by the server and both are sent back to the server on every request.","passage_byte_start":554,"proposed_evidence":"These kinds of query strings are very similar to cookies in that both contain arbitrary pieces of information chosen by the server and both are sent back to the server on every request.","proposed_evidence_chars":185},"initial_reason":"AI semantic entailment confirmed against original source quotation","initial_status":"accepted","model_output_excerpt":"{\n  \"claim\": {\n    \"subject\": \"query string\",\n    \"relation\": \"is similar to\",\n    \"object\": \"cookies\",\n    \"evidence_text\": \"These kinds of query strings are very similar to cookies in that both contain arbitrary pieces of information chosen by the server and both are sent back to the server on every request.\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.98,\n    \"question_relevance\": \"direct\",\n    \"valid_from\": null,\n    \"valid_until\": null\n  }\n}","question":"missing information about the user's specific query","reason":"AI semantic entailment confirmed against original source quotation","relevance":"direct","review_guidance":null,"selected_passage_chars":1769,"selected_passage_excerpt":"Section: URL (query string)\n\n#### URL (query string)\n\nThe query string part of the URL is the part that is typically used for this purpose, but other parts can be used as well.\nThe Java Servlet and PHP session mechanisms both use this method if cookies are not enabled.\n\nThis method consists of the web server appending query strings containing a unique session identifier to all the links inside of a web page.\nWhen the user follows a link, the browser sends the query string to the server, allowing the server to identify the user and maintain state.\n\nThese kinds of query strings are very similar to cookies in that both contain arbitrary pieces of information chosen by the server and both are sent back to the server on every request.\nSince a query string is part of a URL, if that URL is later reused, the same attached piece of information will be sent to the server, which could lead to confusion.\nFor example, if the preferences of a user are encoded in the query string of a URL and the user sends this URL to another user by e-mail, those preferences will be used for that other user as well.\n\nMoreover, if the same user accesses the same page multiple times from different sources, there is no guarantee that the same query string will be used each time.\nFor example, if a user visits a page by coming from a page internal to the site the first time, and then visits the same page by coming from an external search engine the second time, the query strings would likely be different.\nIf cookies were used in this situation, the cookies would be the same.\n\nOther drawbacks of query strings are related to security.\nStoring data that identifies a session in a query string enables session fixation attacks, referer logging attacks and other security exploits.","selected_passage_truncated":false,"semantic_support":{"evidence_text":"These kinds of query strings are very similar to cookies in that both contain arbitrary pieces of information chosen by the server and both are sent back to the server on every request.","model_verdict":"entailed","object":"cookies","reason":"The source explicitly states query strings are very similar to cookies.","relation":"is similar to","stance_override":null,"subject":"query string","valid_from":null,"valid_until":null,"verdict":"entailed"},"source_title":"HTTP cookie","status":"accepted"},"event":"scholar.extraction_decision","ts_unix_ms":1791233377227}
