far more reactive than radon, oganesson may be even more reactive than the elements flerovium and copernicium, which are heavier homologs of the more chemically active elements lead and mercury, respectively.\nThe reason for the possible enhancement of the chemical activity of oganesson relative to radon is an energetic destabilization and a radial expansion of the last occupied 7p-subshell.\nNevertheless, quantum electrodynamic corrections have been shown to be quite significant in reducing this affinity by decreasing the binding in the anion Og^{−} by 9%, thus confirming the importance of these corrections in superheavy elements.","selected_passage_truncated":false,"semantic_support":{"evidence_text":"Oganesson is a member of group 18, the zero-valence elements.","model_verdict":"entailed","object":"zero-valence element","reason":"Source explicitly states Oganesson is a member of the zero-valence elements group.","relation":"is a","stance_override":null,"subject":"Oganesson","valid_from":null,"valid_until":null,"verdict":"entailed"},"source_title":"Oganesson","status":"accepted"},"event":"scholar.extraction_decision","ts_unix_ms":1791233687137}
